args.prompt:
Reference pictures, in this order: ${entry.data.cast}. Keep each character's face, hair, build, clothes and the illustration style exactly as in their reference. Reference pictures other than a character's own are for the place, light and style only: match their room, layout, light and furnishings, and never draw anyone who is in them. The people in this picture are the characters listed below and any figure THIS MOMENT names (a crowd, a skeleton, a stranger); nobody who appears only in a reference picture.

STORY SETTING: ${steps.story.output.rows.0.data.setting}
CHARACTERS (each exactly as in their reference, with these rules): ${steps.cast.output.text}

SCENE: ${steps.scene.output.rows.0.data.place}
Light: ${steps.scene.output.rows.0.data.look}
Always on the set: ${steps.scene.output.rows.0.data.set_dressing}

CHANGES THAT HOLD FROM EARLIER BEATS ON (draw them in this picture, whatever the references show): ${steps.changes.output.text|default:"none"}

THIS MOMENT: ${entry.data.picture}
What they are doing and how they look: ${entry.data.expression}
The one prop the frame is about: ${entry.data.focus}

STYLE RULES: ${steps.world.output.rows.0.data.rules}

Vertical portrait composition, in the same palette and style as the references. No readable text or writing anywhere: no alphabet characters, signs or captions.

actions.message:
Make the next ${steps.items.output.count} stills of “${entry.title}”? Each uses OpenAI image credit (one image each; a Redo is one more). They are made together and nothing more is asked per still. Afterwards look at them (the story's Timeline shows each beat's picture) and run “Screenplay: keep the stills that were made”, or set a beat to redo.

They are:
${steps.items.output.text}

Make the next ${steps.items.output.count} sounds of “${entry.title}”? Each uses ElevenLabs sound-effect credit. They are made together and nothing more is asked per sound. Afterwards listen to them (each sound's page plays its take) and run “Screenplay: keep the sounds that were made”, or set a sound to redo. Music is not included: it has its own workflow.

They are:
${steps.items.output.text}

Make the sheet picture for ${entry.title}? This exact prompt is sent to the image model with the world's ${entry.data.base} base sheet (${entry.data.world}-base-${entry.data.base}) and ${input.style_actor}'s sheet as the references:

A character sheet of one actor on a portrait canvas, the four views arranged in a 2 by 2 grid that fills the whole picture, each view large and full length with soft ground shadows on a plain warm cream background: front and three-quarter on the top row, side and back on the bottom row, standing relaxed, the same person in all four. Built on the attached base sheet's proportions and style: keep its construction and scale of head to body. The second attached picture is an existing actor sheet of this world: draw the faces, hands, hair, skin and cloth exactly in its way, as flat soft matte shapes: two small dark dot eyes, a small plain nose, a tiny smile, simple brow lines, no skin pores, no arm hair, no shaded nose, no fine fabric weave; any stubble or beard is one simple flat speckled shape. Never realistic, never glossy. The actor: ${entry.title}, an adult ${entry.data.base}, age ${entry.data.age}. Build: ${entry.data.build} Hair: ${entry.data.hair} Skin tone: ${entry.data.skin_tone} Look: ${entry.data.look} Distinguishing features, the same in every view: ${entry.data.distinguishing_features} Wearing, in every view: ${entry.data.baseline_wardrobe}. Both legs whole, empty hands, no props. ${steps.world.output.rows.0.data.rules}

Picture size: ${input.size|default:"the connection's"}. It uses OpenAI image credit. To change it, deny this, edit the actor's sheet and run again.

Apply ${entry.title} to ${entry.data.story}?

${entry.data.report}

This makes it the story's Fountain, brings it in (nothing already made is lost: beats keep their picture, recording and sounds; new beats are numbered after the beat before), and prints the revision on the script. Approve to apply, deny to keep it a draft.

You write the image prompt for the MASTER picture of a character in a narrated, illustrated video: the one approved picture every still of this character is made from. It goes to an image model together with the actor's reference picture(s). Follow the section “Writing a master prompt” of the playbook, and the world's style rules below.

DOSSIER
ACTOR: ${steps.actor.output.rows.0.title}; age ${steps.actor.output.rows.0.data.age}; base ${steps.actor.output.rows.0.data.base}
Build: ${steps.actor.output.rows.0.data.build}
Hair: ${steps.actor.output.rows.0.data.hair}
Skin: ${steps.actor.output.rows.0.data.skin_tone}
Look: ${steps.actor.output.rows.0.data.look}
Always keep: ${steps.actor.output.rows.0.data.distinguishing_features}
Baseline wardrobe: ${steps.actor.output.rows.0.data.baseline_wardrobe}
Notes: ${steps.actor.output.rows.0.data.notes}

CHARACTER: ${entry.data.role} (${entry.data.age_state}), presence: ${entry.data.presence}
COSTUME: ${entry.data.costume}
PROPS: ${entry.data.props}
RULES FOR EVERY PICTURE OF THIS CHARACTER: ${entry.data.rules}
CHARACTER SHEET (may be empty): ${entry.data.profile|truncate:2500}

STORY: ${steps.story.output.rows.0.title}. ${steps.story.output.rows.0.data.premise} ${steps.story.output.rows.0.data.setting}

STYLE RULES OF THE WORLD: ${steps.world.output.rows.0.data.rules}

EXTRA INSTRUCTIONS FROM THE PERSON: ${input.notes}

Use only what the dossier says; leave out what it does not say. HARD RULES. The master is a plain portrait of the person, never a scene. (1) The person's extra instructions may change the person only (expression, hands, pose, costume detail): leave out any part of them that names a place, weather, scene, other people or an action, and keep the rest. (2) Write every side-specific feature, prop or garment as the character's own anatomical left or right (for example their anatomical left leg), and say it is not to be mirrored. (3) Never add a background, a set or anything the dossier does not say. Where the costume and the actor's features disagree, keep the actor's features. Answer with the prompt and NOTHING else: no introduction, no quotation marks, no heading, no code fence.
